        },
        {
          "text": "When a shard key has high-frequency values, a compound key that adds a unique or low-frequency field can help. Queries that include a prefix of the shard key (such as a tenantId prefix) can be routed to a specific shard or set of shards, so tenant-only queries may reach more than one shard if a tenant's ranges span shards. A compound hashed key can combine a non-hashed prefix with one hashed field. Sources: https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/sharding-choose-a-shard-key/ ; https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/sharded-cluster-query-router/ ; https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/hashed-sharding/",
          "source": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/sharding-choose-a-shard-key/",
