            "claim_text_sha256": "825d0b625236ce8ce4857f6c048937ddcac98574ddb23e7e4bf0660ec050313b"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "Hashed sharding gives a more even data distribution at the cost of targeted operations: ranged queries on the hashed field are more likely to be broadcast, though equality matches can be targeted to a single shard. Hashing suits monotonically changing values such as ObjectId or timestamps; hashing tenantId alone still gives each tenant one shard-key value (one chunk per value), so it cannot split an unusually large tenant. A compound hashed key with a non-hashed prefix and one hashed field is supported, with the prefix supporting zone ranges and the hashed field giving even distribution. A key that omits the queried field, such as hashed _id, is broadcast for queries that lack the shard key. Sources: https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/hashed-sharding/ ; https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/sharding-choose-a-shard-key/",
          "source": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/hashed-sharding/",
          "note": null,
