        },
        {
          "text": "For a high-frequency shard key value such as a tenant ID, MongoDB recommends a compound key that adds a unique or low-frequency field, since a chunk holding a single shard key value cannot be split. Yorkie's MongoDB sharding design documents a similar pattern: after limited project_id values concentrated data on a few chunks, it proposes a composite shard key (project_id, key) so large chunks can be split by key values. Sources: https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/sharding-choose-a-shard-key.md ; https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/sharding-data-partitioning.md ; https://github.com/yorkie-team/yorkie/issues/673",
          "source": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/sharding-choose-a-shard-key.md",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:89354348682a4a934bc991546d26787daf2bfb7806f693956551c62b9ce02d4c",
          "validationStatus": "REMEDIATED-PROJECTION",
          "evidenceIds": [],
          "also": [
            "https://github.com/yorkie-team/yorkie/blob/main/docs/design/mongodb-sharding.md",
            "https://dbhelm.com/learn/mongodb/lessons/choosing-shard-keys",
            "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/sharding-data-partitioning.md",
