            "claim_text_sha256": "a1d5f9a3dfc06122a28b5f7fef876d9ccc971a9408815d9051e232d09f031803"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "A non-MongoDB outlier-handling example is Datadog Husky: it assigns a variable shard count per tenant from observed traffic and changes future placements as load changes. This illustrates an application-managed placement strategy, not behavior provided by MongoDB's native shard key. (low confidence)",
          "source": "https://www.datadoghq.com/blog/engineering/husky-deep-dive/",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:d1fa8feb04e2c45687bd785a96325c02601893f21752a117f393cd23ddf6c3b9",
          "validationStatus": "SUPPORTED",
          "evidenceIds": [
            "ev:d86922939716cfb267cb0ba82ef0e5609a59f353e0d9e30c2e668017d22de1bb"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "MongoDB zones can constrain shard-key ranges to designated shards for deliberate placement. For tenant placement, the range boundaries must use fields in the shard key (for a compound key, the range must include the key prefix); if the key field is hashed, zone ranges refer to hashed values and do not have predictable routing relative to the original tenantId values. Sources: https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/zone-sharding/",
          "source": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/zone-sharding/",
          "note": null,
          "claimId": "clm:ca13cebfffe992b8aa3cac0fb6bf37f40e9104d5e6a1bbbd0d5b238d511c42a8",
          "validationStatus": "REMEDIATED-PROJECTION",
          "evidenceIds": [],
          "also": [
